WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Lawsuit Claims Big Problem With Popular Arizona Iced Tea Flavor

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Ginseng is rare, expensive, and takes a long time to mature. 

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:22.000
But a 23-fluid-ounce (680 ml) can of Arizona Iced Tea's "Green Tea with Ginseng and Honey" only costs 99 cents or less.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
If you feel something's not quite adding up here, you're not alone. 

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:38.000
As reported in the agricultural magazine Modern Farmer, a new lawsuit claims that the drink contains no detectable ginseng.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:47.000
According to the suit, filed in New York, three separate lab tests could detect no ginseng in the Arizona Iced Tea.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.000
But they did detect ginseng in two competing tea products that claim to include ginseng.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:09.000
If it succeeds, the lawsuit could force the Arizona Iced Tea company to change the labeling for the drink, which the beverage company claims, is the "best-selling green tea" in the US.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Currently, the label has "ginseng extract" second-to-last on its list of ingredients, which are listed from most to least plentiful.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
The tea also claims to contain "ginseng for energy." 

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:38.000
The ginseng root, which is commonly found in China and South Korea but is also grown in the US, has been used in traditional Chinese medicine for thousands of years.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:53.000
It is said to have all sorts of health benefits and typically sells for $50 per pound (.45 kilograms) for the cultivated version. Wild ginseng can go for as high as a thousand bucks per pound.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:03.000
The lawsuit was filed by two consumers and seeks class-action status to represent anyone who has purchased the tea. 

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:15.000
The article points out that McDonald's was once successfully sued for $5 million over just two slices of cheese.
